A CR3 write retires the TLB instead of clearing it; a miss reuses the
retired entry when the page tables still give that translation.

Windows 98 reloads CR3 with its own value to flush its TLB after every
page its VMM maps or unmaps -- one map/unmap pair per page fault, and
3DMark 99 commits and frees buffers every frame: 2,400 flushes a second
in the first-person test, each throwing away every softmmu TLB entry and
followed by ~185 page walks for pages whose mapping nothing had changed
(4.2 million refills per ten seconds).

cpu_x86_update_cr3 now calls tlb_flush_retiring(): the entries filled
since the last flush are copied into a persistent retired table (per mmu
index, the live table's size, accumulating across flushes, since the
guest touches a different few hundred pages in each 0.4 ms interval), and
the flush clears only those filled entries instead of a 64 KiB memset.
On a miss, tlb_fill_align() probes the retired table first and asks the
target -- TCGCPUOps.tlb_retired_reusable, i386: the paging key (CR3, the
paging mode, A20, SMM, the protection keys) recorded at fill time must
equal today's, and every page-table entry the walk read, kept by host
address and value in CPUTLBEntryFull.extra.x86, must still hold that
value -- and puts the entry back with the dirty-tracking flag recomputed.
A flush for any other reason (memory topology, a watchpoint, reset, CR0 /
CR4 / EFER) drops the retired tables, and an invlpg drops its page.
`info jit` prints the refills and how many came back this way, and the
queue's accelerator switches.

Measured: 95 % of the refills reused (3.95 of 4.18 million per ten
seconds); first person 19.0 fps against 18.9 with `tlb-retire=off`, CPU
3DMarks 16295 against 16080 -- inside run-to-run noise on the Ryzen,
where a page walk was cheap; kept for hosts and guests where it is not,
exact by construction.  Switch: `-accel tcg,tlb-retire=off`.
Batteries identical, scripts/test.sh all green.

A flush for any other reason drops the retired table for good, not just
its flag: the next retiring flush copies only the entries filled since
then over their slots and sets the flag again, which resurrected every
older entry. Those flushes exist because the host side of a translation
changed while the page tables and the key did not -- a memory topology
change, a CR0/CR4 write -- so nothing in the table may come back. Windows
XP's VESA mode set on the inbox VGA driver was that: the VGA core swapped
its A0000 window between the chain-4 RAM alias and MMIO (a topology
flush), the next mov cr3 (its int10 call's own TLB flush) revived the
entries filled under the old window, and the mode set ended on a black
screen, or in a text mode with the BIOS's writes gone to stale host
pages. A fresh XP install's resolution change and its display-driver
install (the same VGA reset) both showed it; a mode change that leaves
the window alone did not.

